  "content": {
    "id": "p7-q20-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "20",
    "prompt": "Suppose $m,n\\in\\mathbb{N}$ are coprime. Prove that if $a\\equiv b\\pmod m$ and $a\\equiv b\\pmod n$, then $a\\equiv b\\pmod{mn}$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$a\\equiv b\\pmod{mn}$.\n\nBoth $m$ and $n$ divide $a-b$; since they are coprime, their product divides $a-b$.",
      "worked": "The given congruences mean that\n$$\\begin{align*}a-b&=km,\\\\a-b&=\\ell n\\end{align*}$$\nfor integers $k,\\ell$. Thus $n$ divides $km$. Since $m$ and $n$ are coprime, $n$ divides $k$, so $k=pn$ for some integer $p$. Hence\n$$\\begin{align*}a-b&=(pn)m\\\\a-b&=pmn.\\end{align*}$$\nTherefore $a-b$ is a multiple of $mn$, giving $a\\equiv b\\pmod{mn}$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 40
  },
